WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.230
Behind me is Lake Constance.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:05.170
It is a big lake, near the Alps, in the middle
of Europe.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.750
This bit of me is in Germany, and this bit
of me,

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.420
on the other side of that sculpture, is in
Switzerland.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.420
...they took down the border fence and they
replaced it with sculptures.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.050
A few miles away behind me, over that horizon,
is Austria.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.340
And all three countries have a shoreline on
Lake Constance.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.850
And if you look at a map,

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:23.550
you will notice that something is missing:

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.140
the lines that denote
where the international borders,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.390
the lines that I'm standing on right now,
stop at the lakeshore.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.559
Lake Constance is a condominium,

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:36.070
which is a fancy way of saying that it belongs
to more than one country.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.160
But no-one is entirely sure who.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.760
We are used to the idea that the Earth is
divided up,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.850
and different countries
own different bits of it.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.530
Which is kind of a strange idea when it's
put like that,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.920
but since most of the world agrees on it,
we stick with it.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.370
Laws, taxes, rights and responsibilities change
at formal, defined lines.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.559
Okay, there are international waters,

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:00.699
and Antarctica's a special case, and okay,
there is that one bit of Africa

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:04.350
where two countries both don't want to claim
it, but generally:

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.990
each bit of the world is owned by one particular
nation.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.369
And sure, sometimes borders get complicated
because of treaty glitches or land disputes,

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:15.790
but this isn't one of those: it's just that
no-one ever bothered

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.840
to actually put down in an international treaty
where the borders are.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.689
According to Austria, over there,

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:23.819
Lake Constance is a true condominium,

00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:27.020
shared equally by all three countries all
the way across.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:33.649
If you're on the lake, you are simultaneously
in Germany, and Austria, and Switzerland.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:35.829
Switzerland is not happy with that:

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:39.280
according to them, the border runs straight
down the middle of the lake.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.200
And Germany is basically like, [shrug].

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.600
No-one really has a problem with this.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.850
If there are any legal issues,

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.319
they're settled on a case-by-case basis.

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:51.249
So if you're wondering what happens if someone
gives birth in the middle of the lake,

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:53.520
and what citizenship their kid would have,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.980
or what would happen under salvage laws

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.260
if
someone pulls up sunken treasure for the middle of the--

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.920
I mean, the answer is: [shrug].

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.119
We'll sort it out when we have to,

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:07.179
and in the meantime no-one's really going
to mind.

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:11.560
The only people that this lack of defined
border does affect are cartographers, map-makers:

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.000
there is no option on most digital maps for
[shrug].

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.919
Computers and people
don't know where to draw the line.

